        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state television and IRNA opened Friday evening broadcasts with extended footage of hundreds of thousands of mourners filling Tehran's Azadi Square for the mass funeral procession of "the supreme martyr Ayatollah Khamenei." IRIB anchors described the crowds as "the largest gathering in the history of the Islamic Republic" and presented the turnout as confirmation that "the bond between the nation and the Velayat is unbreakable." The IRGC warning of full-scale offensive operations against American forces was carried as the lead military story, characterized as "the decisive will of the Resistance system in the face of criminal Zionist-American aggression."
      → supports <span class="cite">F8</span>; <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Correspondents made no reference to the economic costs of the Hormuz shutdown; a separate feature segment discussed barter-trade agreements with "friendly states" as evidence that "the maximum-pressure embargo is collapsing." On the nuclear file, unnamed AEOI scientists were cited as stating "the program remains intact and fully protected," with no specifics on which facilities remained operational after seven nights of US strikes.
      → supports <span class="cite">F10</span>; <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Press TV's English service carried an opinion article asserting that Mojtaba Khamenei, "even in his period of recovery," was "exercising direct command over the IRGC and the Resistance Axis." The Knesset dissolution was framed on official broadcasts as confirmation that "the Netanyahu regime is approaching its political end, as the Zionist entity's own parliament confirms its failures."
      → supports <span class="cite">F12</span>; <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

